===Pronunciation===
{{pt-IPA}}
* {{IPA|pt|[ˈmõ.tɪ]|[ˈmõ.tʲ]|a=Northeast Brazil}}
* {{hyphenation|pt|mon|te}}

===Etymology 1===
{{dercat|pt|itc-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
{{inh+|pt|roa-opt|monte|g=m}}, from {{inh|pt|la|mons|montem|g=m}}. Compare {{cog|gl|monte|g=m}}.

====Noun====
{{pt-noun|m}}

# [[mount]]; a [[mountain]] or large [[hill]]
#: {{syn|pt|montanha<t:mountain>|morro<t:large hill>}}
# [[pile]]; [[heap]] {{gloss|a mass of things placed together so as to form a mass or elevation}}
#: {{syn|pt|pilha|amontoado}}
# {{lb|pt|usually in {{l|pt|um monte de|gloss=a lot of}}}} [[heap]]; [[load]]; [[pile]] {{gloss|a great amount of something}}
#: {{syn|pt|amontoado|pilha|penca}}
#: {{uxi|pt|Eu conheco um '''monte''' de gente.|I know a '''lot of''' people.}}
# {{lb|pt|inheritance law}} a share of personal property given to someone as part of an [[inheritance]], or the property as a whole; [[legacy]]
# {{lb|pt|Alentejo}} a rural property or group of farm buildings

=====Usage notes=====
''Monte'' is usually used in reference to elevations smaller than {{l|pt|montanha|montanhas|mountains}}. However, ''monte'' (and not ''montanha'') is used in the full title of mountains regardless of size: ''Monte Evereste'', ''Monte Aconcágua''.

